{
  "result": "Most people think the ocean is one thing, one vast, unbroken blue on the map. But standing at its edge, you realize it is a thousand different places stitched together.\n\nThe water by your feet is warm, lazy, and clear enough to see tiny crabs darting sideways. Ten feet out, it turns a deeper green, choppy, impatient. Further still, it becomes navy, cold, and utterly indifferent to you. The ocean does not care if you are there. That is part of its comfort.\n\nWe spend our lives surrounded by things that react to us - phones that light up when we touch them, people who smile when we smile, doors that open when we pull. The ocean offers no such feedback. You can shout at it, weep into it, throw stones into it, and it will continue its ancient rhythm, breathing in and out without pause.\n\nMaybe that is why we return to it. Not to be seen, but to be allowed to be small for a while. To stand before something so large and old that our worries shrink to their proper size. Your deadlines, your heartbreaks, your embarrassment from three years ago - all of them are still real, but they are not the whole world. The tide reminds you of that.\n\nIt will come in whether you are ready or not. It will go out again. And if you leave your footprints in the wet sand, it will gently, without malice, wipe them clean.\n\nYou get to begin again. And that, perhaps, is the most merciful gift of all.",
